1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,095
Lesson 11.2.

2
00:00:02,475 --> 00:00:05,445
Torx: stochastic
differentiable programming.

3
00:00:06,550 --> 00:00:09,768
THRML simulates block sampling.

4
00:00:10,148 --> 00:00:15,227
But many algorithms are more general:
chains of stochastic transitions with

5
00:00:15,227 --> 00:00:19,899
parameters we want to train, like
a diffusion model or a random walk.

6
00:00:20,279 --> 00:00:25,343
In August twenty twenty-six, Extropic
published parametrized stochastic

7
00:00:25,343 --> 00:00:30,621
circuits and torx, a JAX library to
build, execute and differentiate them.

8
00:00:31,001 --> 00:00:34,689
The idea is the same as with
quantum circuits: a gate

9
00:00:34,689 --> 00:00:38,168
language close to what the
hardware does natively.

10
00:00:39,283 --> 00:00:43,963
A circuit has typed wires:
binary, categorical or continuous.

11
00:00:44,343 --> 00:00:48,001
Each gate is a local stochastic
matrix with parameters.

12
00:00:48,381 --> 00:00:54,445
The simplest is the probabilistic NOT,
PNOT: it flips a pbit with probability p.

13
00:00:54,825 --> 00:00:57,070
It is a mixture of the
identity and the NOT.

14
00:00:57,450 --> 00:01:00,396
The probabilistic swap
and the probabilistic

15
00:01:00,396 --> 00:01:02,939
controlled NOT follow the same recipe.

16
00:01:03,319 --> 00:01:05,775
The complete circuit is the product of its

17
00:01:05,775 --> 00:01:08,114
gates applied to the input distribution.

18
00:01:09,217 --> 00:01:13,450
We reproduce the paper's first
example with our own minimal

19
00:01:13,450 --> 00:01:17,109
NumPy implementation,
written from its definitions.

20
00:01:17,489 --> 00:01:23,578
A PNOT, a swap and a controlled NOT
act on two pbits starting at zero zero.

21
00:01:23,958 --> 00:01:28,880
We want the distribution half zero
zero, half one one, perfect correlation.

22
00:01:29,260 --> 00:01:35,217
After three thousand Adam steps,
the loss falls from 0.33 to 2·10⁻⁵,

23
00:01:35,217 --> 00:01:40,562
and the final distribution is
0.498 in each correlated state.

24
00:01:41,683 --> 00:01:45,227
In hardware we do not have the
exact distribution, only samples.

25
00:01:45,607 --> 00:01:48,792
For mixture gates with p = σ(θ),

26
00:01:49,172 --> 00:01:52,751
the derivative of the gate is
the difference of two gates of

27
00:01:52,751 --> 00:01:56,091
the same family: the original
and another with a shifted

28
00:01:56,091 --> 00:01:59,550
parameter, whose value of p
is the square of the original.

29
00:01:59,930 --> 00:02:03,180
We verify the identity with an
error of ten to the minus eleven.

30
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:07,007
The gradient is estimated
with the difference of two

31
00:02:07,007 --> 00:02:10,785
averages of circuit runs: it
is the parameter-shift rule.

32
00:02:11,917 --> 00:02:14,716
We compare with REINFORCE,
the score-function

33
00:02:14,716 --> 00:02:18,201
estimator, which uses the
circuit's internal trajectory.

34
00:02:18,581 --> 00:02:22,795
With a thousand runs, both
are unbiased: the exact

35
00:02:22,795 --> 00:02:26,504
gradient is −0.0428,
and the averages agree.

36
00:02:26,884 --> 00:02:29,960
In this example, REINFORCE
had lower variance.

37
00:02:30,340 --> 00:02:34,214
The advantage of the parameter
shift is different: it only needs

38
00:02:34,214 --> 00:02:37,665
to run the circuit and read
the output, without observing

39
00:02:37,665 --> 00:02:41,480
intermediate states, which on a
chip are not always accessible.

40
00:02:42,583 --> 00:02:46,295
Another family of gates comes
from exponentiating a rate matrix.

41
00:02:46,675 --> 00:02:51,233
PIsing applies for a time the Glauber
dynamics of two coupled spins.

42
00:02:51,613 --> 00:02:57,102
From the uniform distribution, with
coupling one and beta two, at time five

43
00:02:57,102 --> 00:03:02,005
it reaches the Boltzmann distribution:
0.491 in the aligned states.

44
00:03:02,385 --> 00:03:05,828
But careful: applying PIsing
edge by edge on a ring of

45
00:03:05,828 --> 00:03:09,079
eight spins does not sample
the ring's Ising model.

46
00:03:09,460 --> 00:03:12,763
In our case it stays at a total variation

47
00:03:12,763 --> 00:03:16,709
distance of 0.84, while
chromatic Gibbs is exact.

48
00:03:17,089 --> 00:03:19,112
The paper finds the same effect.

49
00:03:20,217 --> 00:03:24,263
Stochastic circuits are useful
for random walks, discrete

50
00:03:24,263 --> 00:03:28,523
diffusion, stochastic networks
on graphs and jump diffusion.

51
00:03:28,903 --> 00:03:34,264
The same circuit runs with software or
hardware randomness: the paper uses pbits

52
00:03:34,264 --> 00:03:38,821
from the X0 chip and obtains estimates
consistent with the baseline.

53
00:03:39,201 --> 00:03:43,825
And when compiling, use native
Gibbs gates, not edge-tiled PIsing.

54
00:03:44,950 --> 00:03:47,625
Common mistakes: assuming
that composing local

55
00:03:47,625 --> 00:03:49,951
equilibria gives the global equilibrium.

56
00:03:50,331 --> 00:03:54,041
Using REINFORCE when the internal
states cannot be observed.

57
00:03:54,421 --> 00:03:58,591
Pushing probabilities to zero or
one, where the score diverges.

58
00:03:58,971 --> 00:04:04,067
And ignoring the calibration error
of real pbits, around five percent.

59
00:04:05,183 --> 00:04:09,318
In summary, a parametrized
stochastic circuit composes typed

60
00:04:09,318 --> 00:04:13,522
local gates, and torx builds,
executes and differentiates it.

61
00:04:13,902 --> 00:04:17,117
The shift identity turns
the derivative into the

62
00:04:17,117 --> 00:04:21,068
difference of two runs, useful
when we only see the output.

63
00:04:21,448 --> 00:04:25,506
Generator-derived gates, like
PIsing, are powerful, but composing

64
00:04:25,506 --> 00:04:28,939
them edge by edge does not
give the global equilibrium.

65
00:04:30,050 --> 00:04:33,089
To practice: write the PNOT matrix.

66
00:04:33,469 --> 00:04:36,087
Prove the relation of
the shifted parameter.

67
00:04:36,467 --> 00:04:40,011
Explain why the parameter
shift suits hardware.

68
00:04:40,391 --> 00:04:43,313
And explain why edge-tiled
PIsing is not Gibbs.
